{:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274224} {:answer/id #uuid "f06e744b-0c4e-56b4-9ccf-073c04c36996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274515} {:answer/id #uuid "f7eafda1-95e4-50d3-9e5d-c6169be19dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "134" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274202} :answer-field/id #uuid "f95e766c-71b6-5648-a6e3-04cdaca450da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275075}] :question/id #uuid "c3a8456a-5f67-54bf-8d0a-07a8bbe82bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158114" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6(4-25) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|126|=126.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e51492-c3b7-54b6-8660-3327203595bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272699} {:answer/id #uuid "28eeb732-2c18-5922-b6a9-bbd2d7eec5d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272751} {:answer/id #uuid "3084793d-d7d4-5080-9297-c5dbc7fc686c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272817} {:answer/id #uuid "b981ba03-1c73-5fee-958e-9f516f79590f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273996} {:answer/id #uuid "ed17d628-c951-506b-b286-13e3eb6a5ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272699} :answer-field/id #uuid "56164ca7-3d80-5b0c-bf3e-27409397895a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274782}] :question/id #uuid "c594e512-d19a-53c5-8f50-4caaf295111c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158368" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $3$rd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-120|=120.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049a84c1-52fe-5a96-80d0-bdfba1ece812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272428} {:answer/id #uuid "2e002483-bff0-5696-8b17-34e6d65302e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272797} {:answer/id #uuid "650c50e4-830c-5672-a10f-2d86d1f80ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273251} {:answer/id #uuid "686c0b94-d2c7-570f-a49e-0b44f7a5e887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273289} {:answer/id #uuid "f965d840-d861-5dd1-be3a-b2a400272e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274574} :answer-field/id #uuid "6428d2da-cec3-5130-8fdf-f3195ddd3a25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274807}] :question/id #uuid "5d7bd54b-d293-5ee2-b790-9af4e0cb0ced" :question/math-academy-id "q-158369" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3=-45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-45|=45.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb8b630-1858-5675-a8ea-afd745123540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272641} {:answer/id #uuid "25136c9e-920f-566e-a408-ea4a7af1ef37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272712} {:answer/id #uuid "28f41079-6423-5124-b7dd-2b748bc5ac50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272753} {:answer/id #uuid "37c41e9d-c90b-5532-80b8-70fb57bf191a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272875} {:answer/id #uuid "39e96dc4-16ab-55e3-b742-85d0597e033b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272890} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a3f55ee-40e6-566b-9375-89a8f6437390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274708}] :question/id #uuid "94fb05dd-7b6c-5fef-becd-0abc3ccf0dc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158371" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $1$st row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-14+30) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|128|=128.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ca3fc2b-bad0-5b4b-bc00-408085561cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369847} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c7791b-9b12-5dd6-9d19-547ef87dd6a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369848} {:answer/id #uuid "c232025a-ca14-5a4a-9e9f-a8ff81b74a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369849} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6c71c2-2201-52f2-abf4-3b33cdc6feea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369850} {:answer/id #uuid "1571cd95-16d4-5e7a-a516-2378f040ff5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369847} :answer-field/id #uuid "10725161-b676-53dc-8bff-5c967c1e8dfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369846}] :question/id #uuid "9fc304b0-a53d-5a62-b8cd-1ca341a43179" :question/math-academy-id "q-158367" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $4$th row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-12|=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da1aec45-a768-5bc0-8b78-ec818aed675a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369854} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5fcf24-0e46-5b4b-9678-6276ff3944ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369855} {:answer/id #uuid "326d3d9a-2161-5ea5-a630-9abd2df1cb38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369856} {:answer/id #uuid "c014177e-0ec9-57d0-9ab5-61d474743308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369857} {:answer/id #uuid "12bbbdba-f568-5df7-bc40-4fdfb1daeda6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369857} :answer-field/id #uuid "190a5c0e-6b81-53d6-b669-800a2e45a632" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369853}] :question/id #uuid "47b0b8bf-3751-5718-9c72-c6b036aa4e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-158370" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(15+21) \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|144|=144.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3194d732-4a27-57e4-90b5-376278b1fad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429806} {:answer/id #uuid "3c025708-e1f6-590a-bdc9-0995f51cbaec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429807} {:answer/id #uuid "a745ff62-0494-5386-bded-3bb0f59f39cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429810} {:answer/id #uuid "f1560774-e64f-5713-95be-58cfbace4e0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429813} {:answer/id #uuid "fb522c79-1eec-53f4-a1ce-a41acd3ad978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429813} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b729052-de1a-56fa-9f79-cc8f868046e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429815}] :question/id #uuid "8d62e579-1b65-5746-996a-3e6e877f180c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158107" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5 & 13 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5 & 13 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=(-7)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 1=-70.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-70|=70.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of an N-Dimensional Parallelepiped"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1771 :topic/title "Basic Properties of Determinants"}]}
